    "statement0": "<p style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\">The word <span  \"><i>\"assimilated\" </i></span> is mentioned two or more times in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0.0%</span> of neuroscience papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. </p>Among 2020-2024 papers containing <span  \"><i>\"assimilated\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">50.6%</span> of reported significant p-values (p < .05) are fragile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a far higher rate than the median Psychology paper (median shown in black); relative to other words, papers with <span  \"><i>\"assimilated\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">100<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages (lower = better). <br>Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability.",
    "statement1": "As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span  \"><i>\"assimilated\" </i></span> tend to be published in lower impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0<sup>th</sup></span> percentile among all words)  but tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">99<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are less likely to publish papers containing <span  \"><i>\"assimilated\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0<sup>th</sup></span> percentile). "
